 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The cache uses a 2Q replacement policy to prevent sequential screenings from flushing frequently reused comparison sheets. New sheets enter an initial FIFO queue ($A1_{in}$) on first access. Entries evicted from $A1_{in}$ have their identifiers retained in an address ghost queue ($A1_{out}$) without retaining file bytes. If an access hits an identifier in $A1_{out}$, the newly rendered or readmitted sheet is placed into the hot queue ($Am$), which is managed by LRU. Re-accesses to entries already in $Am$ move them to the head of $Am$. Evictions to satisfy the 12 GiB disk bound occur from $A1_{in}$ and $Am$.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q | LRU, Frequency-based with aging | 2Q isolates sequential one-off screening scans in $A1_{in}$ while preserving frequently compared sheets in $Am$, without the slow adaptation or counter maintenance of frequency schemes. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. Eviction policy: 2Q selected to protect comparison sheets from sequential scans.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Initial size split between $A1_{in}$ and $Am$ (e.g., 25% $A1_{in}$ / 75% $Am$) and capacity tuning of $A1_{out}$ based on pilot access metrics.
